window.docContents[91].resolve({"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Script-API-Reference--Accessing-the-Environment":{"contents":"Monads that provide access to information about the current Lake environment (such as the locations of Lean, Lake, and other tools) have MonadLakeEnv instances.\nThis is true for all of the monads in the Lake API, including ScriptM.\n\nA monad equipped with a (read-only) detected environment for Lake.\n\nGets the current Lake environment.\n\nReturns the LAKE_NO_CACHE/--no-cache Lake configuration.\n\nReturns whether the LAKE_NO_CACHE/--no-cache Lake configuration is NOT set.\n\nReturns the LAKE_PACKAGE_URL_MAP for the Lake environment. Empty if none.\n\nReturns the name of Elan toolchain for the Lake environment. Empty if none.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Build Tools\u0009Lake\u0009Script API Reference","header":"24.1.4.1. Accessing the Environment","id":"/Build-Tools-and-Distribution/Lake/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Build-Tools-and-Distribution--Lake--Script-API-Reference--Accessing-the-Environment"},"/IO/Async/#selector-protocol-cleanup":{"contents":"When the race is completed, selectors are offered the opportunity to clean up.\nThis occurs no matter whether the race has been won or a  Selector.registerFn threw an exception, but not when the initial tryFn loop returned a value.\nIn this phase, Selector.unregisterFn is called on every selector in the array, regardless of whether it was registered in the registration phase or whether it threw an exception during registration.\n\nCleanup always occurs, regardless of whether an error occurred during registration, racing, or in prior selectors' unregisterFn implementations.\nSelectors must therefore be written so that unregisterFn is safe to use even when their registerFn lost the race, was never called, or threw an exception.\n\nNatural Number TickerA natTicker is a selector that makes a Nat available every 100 milliseconds, incrementing each time.\nIts state is determined by two values:1. a counter, which is an IO.Ref that contains the next Nat to emit2. the time at which the process was startedThe Selector.tryFn checks whether at least 100ms have elapsed for each emitted Nat.\nIf so, the value is incremented and returned immediately:def tickerTryFn (counter : IO.Ref Nat) (startMs : Nat) := do\n  let nowMs ← IO.monoMsNow\n  let n ← counter.get\n  if nowMs ≥ startMs + n * 100 then\n    counter.set (n + 1)\n    return (some n)\n  else\n    return none\nIf the race was not immediately run, a waiter is registered.\nA background task is launched that sleeps until the next Nat is ready, and the waiter's race is invoked; if the race is won, then the counter is incremented:def tickerRegisterFn (counter : IO.Ref Nat) (startMs : Nat)\n    (waiter : Waiter Nat) : Async Unit := do\n  let n ← counter.get\n  let delay := startMs + n * 100 - (← IO.monoMsNow)\n  let sleep ← Sleep.mk <| .ofNat delay\n  discard <| background do\n    sleep.wait\n    waiter.race (pure ()) fun promise => do\n      counter.set (n + 1)\n      promise.resolve (.ok n)\nThese components can be combined into a selector:def natTicker : IO (Selector Nat) := do\n  let current ← IO.mkRef 0\n  let startMs ← IO.monoMsNow\n  return {\n    tryFn := tickerTryFn current startMs\n    registerFn := tickerRegisterFn current startMs\n    unregisterFn := pure ()\n  }\nThis selector is not thread-safe.\nMultiple uses in a single Selectable.one are safe, because they do not lose data (the set is only invoked when the race has been definitively won).\nHowever, concurrent invocations of Selectable.one on the same natTicker can lead to data races.\nFixing this requires careful locking.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009IO\u0009Asynchronous Programming\u0009Event Selection\u0009Selection Protocol","header":"21.12.3.1.3. Cleanup","id":"/IO/Async/#selector-protocol-cleanup"},"/Tactic-Proofs/Targeted-Rewriting-with--conv/#conv-simp":{"contents":"simp [thm] performs simplification using thm and marked @[simp] lemmas.\nSee the simp tactic for more information.\n\ndsimp is the definitional simplifier in conv-mode. It differs from simp in that it only\napplies theorems that hold by reflexivity.Examples:example (a : Nat): (0 + 0) = a - a := by\n  conv =>\n    lhs\n    dsimp\n    rw [← Nat.sub_self a]\n\n\nsimp_match simplifies match expressions. For example,match [a, b] with\n| [] => 0\n| hd :: tl => hd\nsimplifies to a.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Tactic Proofs\u0009Targeted Rewriting with  conv\u0009Changing the Goal","header":"14.6.4.2. Simplification","id":"/Tactic-Proofs/Targeted-Rewriting-with--conv/#conv-simp"},"/The--grind--tactic/Interactive-Mode/#grind-interactive-solvers":{"contents":"Each of these tactics runs one of grind's decision procedures on the current state.\n\nlia linear integer arithmetic.\n\nlinarith linear arithmetic.\n\nring (commutative) rings and fields.\n\nac associativity and commutativity procedure.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009The  grind  tactic\u0009Interactive Mode","header":"16.13.5. Theory Solvers","id":"/The--grind--tactic/Interactive-Mode/#grind-interactive-solvers"}});